    {
      "id": "958990e22c",
      "commit": "958990e22c4ab6daf434d89cf2b86d7a4a7a9e3c",
      "parent": "ed86a1528aa015f219f8d3385ea2ebd3f63a5212",
      "subject": "Raise for negative slice sizes in sliced()",
      "body": "sliced(seq, n) with a negative n silently produced a wrong result\ninstead of raising or returning empty. Because the internal generator\nuses seq[i : i + n] with i stepping by count(0, n), a negative n makes\ntakewhile(len, ...) yield a single truncated slice and stop:\n\n    >>> list(sliced('ABCDEFG', -1))\n    ['ABCDEF']\n\nThis is the worst failure mode: no error, just quietly incorrect\noutput. The documented sibling chunked() already rejects a negative n\n(via islice), and tail() was recently changed to raise for negative\nsizes for the same reason. sliced() is actually worse than pre-fix\ntail(), which returned an empty result rather than wrong data.\n\nGuard n < 0 up front and raise ValueError('n must be at least 0'),\nmatching tail()'s message. n == 0 still returns an empty iterator\n(unchanged), as do all n >= 1 cases. With strict=True the negative\ncase previously raised a misleading \"seq is not divisible by n\"; it\nnow raises the correct domain error before iteration begins.\n\nAdd SlicedTests.test_negative covering both the default and strict\npaths.",
      "source_files": [
        "more_itertools/more.py"
      ],
      "test_files": [
        "tests/test_more.py"
      ],
